A||N||4||peterh||windshield washer reservoir leaks||||1||
Z||000000||peterh||01-17-2001||09:08 AM||||The windshield washer reservoir just started leaking on my car. It has a crack on the botton. What can I use to seal the leak? I was thinking of polyester resin or epoxy with fiberglass but am not sure this would adhere or hold up under changes in temperature.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||01-17-2001||09:27 AM||||The plastic these are amde of is very hard to fix...nothing much sticks to it.<P>I'd try some clear silicone...be sure the cracked area is very clean before you try this.<P>Better yet, get another from the boneyard. Probably any reservoir from a GM car will work.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||01-17-2001||12:06 PM||||The Fiero Store had new ones last year for about $6.||||reg||1
Z||000003||peterh||01-17-2001||01:37 PM||||I called the Fiero store and they do not carry them. I will try the silicone first and then Kickhill for a used one.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||01-17-2001||01:51 PM||||The reservoir is made of either polypropylene of polyehtylene which are both thermoplastics.  This means that you can simply weld the crack shut with a solder iron of a wood burning tool or even a hot ice pick.<P>I've done it to many windshield washer tanks as well as radiator overflow tanks.<P>I use a solder gun and run it along the crack softening the plastic and molding the crack shut as I go.  If you need some filler material shave some off another part of the tank such as where mold flash is.<P>I've also repaired the plastic shields that run underneath the radiator/engine on my daughters' Camry and Protege.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll<p>[This message has been edited by Carrolles (edited 01-17-2001).]||||reg||1
